<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baalat Gebal</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lady of Byblos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baalat Gebal, 'the Lady of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>', was the chief goddess of the Phoenician city of Byblos. Her temple received offerings from Egyptian kings from the Old Kingdom onwards, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> identified her with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathor">Hathor</a>. The kings of Byblos ruled under her protection, as their inscriptions record, and her cult continued into Roman times.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Her name appears in Akkadian in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a> as Bēltu ša Gubla, and she was also known as Baaltis. Researchers once took it to be only an epithet and identified her with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asherah">Asherah</a>, Anat or, most often, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Astarte</a>. The evidence for Astarte rests on three late, unprovenanced inscriptions, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philo-of-byblos">Philo of Byblos</a> makes Astarte and Baaltis separate sisters. Following Anna Elise Zernecke (2013), researchers now take Baalat Gebal as a proper name: Phoenician inscriptions leave it undivided, unlike the title king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>. Egyptians called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathor">Hathor</a> the Lady of Byblos, first in the Coffin Texts, and may later have identified her with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>She was the principal deity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> and was associated with commerce. Her temple in the centre of the city, its largest sanctuary, was in use without interruption from the third millennium BCE to the Roman period. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rib-hadda">Rib-Addi</a>, king of Byblos in the period of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a>, ended most of his letters to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> with a wish that she protect him, twenty-seven times in all, a habit no other ruler in the correspondence shares. One letter names Ummaḫnu, her maidservant and presumably her priestess, who tried to play a part in the city's foreign relations.</p><p>Kings of the first millennium BCE, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abibaal">Abibaal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elibaal">Elibaal</a>, Shipitbaal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yehawmilk">Yehawmilk</a>, called on her for protection and a long reign. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> took part in her cult: an Old Kingdom relief from her temple calls a king beloved of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathor">Hathor</a>, Lady of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>, and a stone table records an endowment under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepi-i">Pepi I</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>She appears in the satirical Letter of Hori, which asks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>, &quot;And their goddess, what is she like?&quot;, but not in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/story-of-wenamun">Story of Wenamun</a>, though it describes a journey to Byblos. Greek writers likened her to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a>. In the Phoenician History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philo-of-byblos">Philo of Byblos</a> she is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dione">Dione</a>, sent with her sisters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Astarte</a> and Rhea to defeat Kronos, who marries them instead, and she later receives Byblos as her domain. Lucian's De Dea Syria calls her the Byblian Aphrodite and says rites of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adonis">Adonis</a> were held in her temple in Roman times.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Baalat Gebal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the centre of the city stood the temple of its goddess, the <b>Baalat Gebal</b>, the Lady of Byblos, whom the Egyptians identified with Hathor.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Byblos honoured <b>Baalat Gebal</b>, “the Lady of Byblos”, whom the Egyptians identified with Hathor.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li></ul></section>
